WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.861
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.161 --> 00:00:09.547
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.847 --> 00:00:16.401
Unit nine: Entertainment News. Today you will describe people and things with relative clauses (who, which).

4
00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:19.214
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.527
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:25.524
Box office.

7
00:00:26.517 --> 00:00:30.571
Box office means the total money a film earns from ticket sales.

8
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:33.624
The film topped the box office this weekend.

9
00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:36.753
Your turn. Say: Box office.

10
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.915
Box office.

11
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.384
Premiere.

12
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:46.451
Premiere means the first public showing of a film or show.

13
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.166
The premiere took place in Phnom Penh.

14
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.263
Your turn. Say: Premiere.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:55.869
Premiere.

16
00:00:56.617 --> 00:00:57.734
Celebrity.

17
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.900
Celebrity means a famous person.

18
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:03.470
The celebrity waved to the crowd.

19
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:06.747
Your turn. Say: Celebrity.

20
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.578
Celebrity.

21
00:01:11.317 --> 00:01:12.492
Award season.

22
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:17.445
Award season means the time of year when film and music prizes are given.

23
00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.479
Award season brings many new dramas.

24
00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:23.666
Your turn. Say: Award season.

25
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.629
Award season.

26
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:29.454
Critic review.

27
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.110
Critic review means an expert's written opinion about a film or show.

28
00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:36.693
A critic review praised the acting.

29
00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:40.261
Your turn. Say: Critic review.

30
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.252
Critic review.

31
00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:46.517
Streaming release.

32
00:01:47.517 --> 00:01:51.142
Streaming release means the day a film becomes available online.

33
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.168
The streaming release reached many viewers.

34
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:57.505
Your turn. Say: Streaming release.

35
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.832
Streaming release.

36
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:04.478
Fan reaction.

37
00:02:05.483 --> 00:02:09.039
Fan reaction means how a film's admirers respond to it.

38
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.661
Fan reaction was loud and joyful.

39
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.169
Your turn. Say: Fan reaction.

40
00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:19.952
Fan reaction.

41
00:02:20.717 --> 00:02:21.683
Publicity.

42
00:02:22.683 --> 00:02:26.471
Publicity means attention given to someone or something by the media.

43
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:29.211
Good publicity helps a new film.

44
00:02:29.817 --> 00:02:33.154
Your turn. Say: Publicity.

45
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.048
Publicity.

46
00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:41.171
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.017 --> 00:02:48.396
Visal, this article says 'The film, which opened on Friday, topped the box office.' Why the commas?

48
00:02:49.483 --> 00:02:58.498
That is a non-defining relative clause. It adds extra information, so we use commas and 'which' for things or 'who' for people.

49
00:02:59.583 --> 00:03:01.014
And without commas?

50
00:03:02.117 --> 00:03:09.239
'The actor who plays the king won an award' is defining. The clause tells us which actor, so we cannot remove it.

51
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.768
Entertainment news uses a lot of these, right?

52
00:03:13.883 --> 00:03:23.095
Yes. They pack detail into one sentence: the celebrity who attended the premiere, the review which praised the music.

53
00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:26.117
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.983 --> 00:03:30.237
Grammar focus: Relative clauses with who and which.

55
00:03:30.537 --> 00:03:31.735
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.692
The film, which opened on Friday, topped the box office.

57
00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:39.024
The actor who plays the king won an award.

58
00:03:39.917 --> 00:03:43.054
A critic review that praised the music pleased the fans.

59
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.946
Use who for people and which for things. Add commas around non-defining clauses (extra information), but not around defining clauses.

60
00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:56.682
Be careful! Don't say: The film who opened Friday won.

61
00:03:58.633 --> 00:04:01.155
Say: The film which opened Friday won.

62
00:04:01.455 --> 00:04:03.757
Use which for things and who for people.

63
00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:07.080
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:09.367
Night at the Premiere.

65
00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:13.536
Crowds gathered at the premiere of a film which has already won praise.

66
00:04:14.533 --> 00:04:19.540
The celebrity who directed it greeted fans, and publicity teams handed out posters.

67
00:04:20.533 --> 00:04:24.414
A critic review published that morning described the story as brave and moving.

68
00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.488
Early box office figures suggest that the film, which cost little to make, will earn much more.

69
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.833
Fan reaction on the streaming release day will decide how long it stays popular.

70
00:04:36.933 --> 00:04:41.260
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:42.117 --> 00:04:44.708
The actor blank plays the king won an award.

72
00:04:45.008 --> 00:04:48.360
A, who. B, which. C, whose is.

73
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.888
The answer is A: who.

74
00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.665
The first public showing of a film is a blank.

75
00:04:57.965 --> 00:05:01.583
A, premiere. B, release. C, publicity.

76
00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:06.843
The answer is A: premiere.

77
00:05:07.983 --> 00:05:11.074
An expert's written opinion about a film is a blank.

78
00:05:11.374 --> 00:05:15.898
A, fan reaction. B, critic review. C, box office.

79
00:05:19.433 --> 00:05:21.967
The answer is B: critic review.

80
00:05:23.117 --> 00:05:24.838
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:25.138 --> 00:05:32.088
A, The film who opened Friday won.. B, The film which opened on Friday won.. C, The film what opened Friday won..

82
00:05:35.633 --> 00:05:39.014
The answer is B: The film which opened on Friday won..

83
00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.997
According to the reading, the film cost…

84
00:05:43.297 --> 00:05:46.776
A, a lot. B, little to make. C, nothing.

85
00:05:50.333 --> 00:05:52.437
The answer is B: little to make.

86
00:05:53.633 --> 00:05:56.831
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:57.683 --> 00:06:01.227
How much should a critic's review influence whether you watch a new film?

88
00:06:10.017 --> 00:06:11.563
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:11.863 --> 00:06:31.448
A critic's review can help, but it should not decide everything. Critics often notice acting and story quality, which saves me from wasting time. However, their taste may differ from mine, so I also check fan reaction and the trailer. In the end, I trust my own interests most.

90
00:06:31.748 --> 00:06:33.504
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:34.417 --> 00:06:39.136
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and relative clauses with who and which.

92
00:06:39.436 --> 00:06:44.781
Today's value is media literacy. Ask who made the message, and why, before you believe it.

93
00:06:45.081 --> 00:06:47.455
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:06:47.755 --> 00:06:51.174
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

